-- title:
--   Two-cusp integral lattice equals the two-cusp integral set
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ with the property that every unit $u \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ whose image under the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times} \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^{\times}$ is trivial lies in $H$. Let $y$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ for the subgroup [`CohCarrier.GammaH M H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) of $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$, namely the image in $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$ of the preimage of $H$ under the character $\Gamma_0(M) \to (\mathbb{Z}/M)^{\times}$ given by the lower-right entry. Take the coefficient ring to be $\bot : \mathrm{Subring}\ \mathbb{C}$, the prime subring of $\mathbb{C}$, i.e. the image of $\mathbb{Z}$. The assertion is: if $y$ lies in [`CuspForm.twoCuspLattice M H 2 p ⊥`](def/CuspForm_TwoCuspLattice.html#L86), the $\bot$-span of the two-cusp integral set, then $y$ lies in that set itself, that is, for every $t$ in the Hecke ring [`CuspForm.heckeRingH M H 2`](def/CuspForm_TwoCuspLattice.html#L37) (the subring of $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphisms of the space of weight-$2$ cusp forms generated by the range of `heckeGenH` for the empty exclusion set), every Atkin–Lehner datum $W$ for $(M,p)$ — a factorisation $M = pR$ together with integers $a,b$ satisfying $pa - Rb = 1$ — and every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion of width $1$ of $t y$ and of $\mathrm{alSlash}_W(t y) = (t y)\mid_2 W$ both lie in $\bot$.
--
--   This is the statement that the two-cusp integral set of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_H(M)$ at $p$ is already a module over the prime subring of $\mathbb{C}$, so that the lattice it spans adds nothing; integrality is tested simultaneously at the cusp $\infty$ and at the cusp obtained by applying an Atkin–Lehner datum. It is used in the construction of the two reduction maps of the two-cusp $q$-expansion principle and in the further integrality statements that depend on them.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CuspForm_mem_twoCuspIntegralSet_of_mem_twoCuspLattice.lean

